<main id="main">
<section class="phero"><div class="phero-media"><img src="/assets/img/photos/laptop-estimate.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/laptop-estimate-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/laptop-estimate.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="626" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.18"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span class="sep" aria-hidden="true">/</span><span aria-current="page">Claim &amp; policy terms</span></nav><p class="kicker">Reference</p><h1>Insurance Claim &amp; Policy Terms Glossary</h1><p class="lede">42 terms you’ll see in policies, estimates and appraisal awards, defined in plain English. Type to filter.</p></div></section><div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose"><div class="filter"><label for="gloss-filter">Search the glossary</label><input id="gloss-filter" type="search" placeholder="Try “depreciation” or “umpire”" autocomplete="off"><p class="filter-count" data-gloss-count aria-live="polite">42 terms</p></div><div class="az"><a href="#g-A">A</a><a href="#g-B">B</a><a href="#g-C">C</a><a href="#g-D">D</a><a href="#g-E">E</a><a href="#g-F">F</a><a href="#g-H">H</a><a href="#g-L">L</a><a href="#g-M">M</a><a href="#g-N">N</a><a href="#g-O">O</a><a href="#g-P">P</a><a href="#g-R">R</a><a href="#g-S">S</a><a href="#g-U">U</a><a href="#g-V">V</a><a href="#g-X">X</a></div><dl class="glossary"><div class="gterm" id="g-A"><dt>Actual cash value (ACV)</dt><dd>Replacement cost minus depreciation. Many policies pay ACV first and the rest after repairs.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Additional living expense (ALE)</dt><dd>Coverage for the extra cost of living elsewhere while a home is being repaired after a covered loss.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Amount of loss</dt><dd>The dollar value of a covered loss. It’s the only question an appraisal panel decides.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Appraisal award</dt><dd>The written decision signed by two of the three panel members that sets the amount of loss.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Appraisal clause</dt><dd>The policy condition that lets either party demand appraisal when they disagree on the amount of loss.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Appraiser</dt><dd>The person each side names to value the loss during appraisal. Usually required to be competent and disinterested.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-B"><dt>Bureau of Insurance</dt><dd>The division of Virginia’s State Corporation Commission that regulates insurers, agents and public adjusters.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-C"><dt>Coinsurance</dt><dd>A commercial policy clause that reduces payment if a building was insured for less than a set percentage of its value.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Collateral damage</dt><dd>Damage to soft metals, screens or other items that helps confirm a storm’s date and severity.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Coverage A</dt><dd>Dwelling coverage on a homeowners policy: the house and attached structures.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Coverage B</dt><dd>Other structures coverage, such as detached garages, sheds and fences.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Coverage C</dt><dd>Personal property coverage for contents.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-D"><dt>Debris removal</dt><dd>Coverage for removing debris after a covered loss. Tree removal often has separate, lower limits.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Declarations page</dt><dd>The summary page of a policy listing the insured, limits, deductibles and endorsements.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Deductible</dt><dd>The share of a covered loss the policyholder pays before the insurer pays.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Depreciation</dt><dd>The reduction in value from age, wear and obsolescence used to calculate actual cash value.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Derecho</dt><dd>A long-lived, widespread windstorm from a line of thunderstorms, such as the June 2012 storm across Virginia.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Disinterested</dt><dd>Having no financial stake in a claim’s outcome. Many appraisal clauses require it of each appraiser.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-E"><dt>Endorsement</dt><dd>A form that adds to, removes or changes the terms of the base policy.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Estimate review</dt><dd>An independent, line-by-line check of an insurer’s estimate against the damage and local pricing.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-F"><dt>FAIR Plan</dt><dd>A state insurance pool for owners who can’t find coverage elsewhere. Virginia’s is the VPIA.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Functional damage</dt><dd>Damage that affects how a material performs, such as water-shedding or service life, as opposed to cosmetic damage.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-H"><dt>Hurricane deductible</dt><dd>A separate deductible, often a percentage of Coverage A, that applies to losses from a hurricane or named storm.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-L"><dt>Like kind and quality</dt><dd>The standard for replacement materials: comparable in type and quality, not necessarily identical.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Line item</dt><dd>A single priced entry in an estimate, such as a quantity of shingles or a room of drywall.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-M"><dt>Matching</dt><dd>Whether new materials can reasonably match undamaged materials, a common dispute after partial damage.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Mitigation</dt><dd>Steps to prevent further damage after a loss, such as tarping or water extraction. Usually required and reimbursable.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-N"><dt>Named storm</dt><dd>A tropical storm or hurricane named by the National Hurricane Center. Often the trigger for a separate deductible.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-O"><dt>Ordinance or law coverage</dt><dd>Coverage for added costs of meeting building codes or local rules during repair, usually with its own limit.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Overhead and profit (O&amp;P)</dt><dd>A general contractor’s markup for coordinating multiple trades, often 10% and 10%.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-P"><dt>Proof of loss</dt><dd>A sworn statement of the amount claimed. Many policies require it within a set time after the insurer requests it.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Public adjuster</dt><dd>A professional licensed by the Bureau of Insurance to handle and negotiate a claim for the policyholder for a fee.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-R"><dt>Recoverable depreciation</dt><dd>Depreciation held back from the first payment and paid once repairs are completed.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Replacement cost value (RCV)</dt><dd>The cost to repair or replace damaged property with like kind and quality, without deducting depreciation.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-S"><dt>Scope of loss</dt><dd>The list of damaged items and the work needed to repair them.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Square</dt><dd>A roofing unit equal to 100 square feet of roof surface.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Steep charge</dt><dd>Extra labor added to an estimate for roofs too steep to walk safely, often 7/12 pitch and up.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Suit limitation</dt><dd>The policy deadline for filing a lawsuit against the insurer, commonly two years in Virginia.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Supplement</dt><dd>A request for additional payment for items missing from or underpriced in the original estimate.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-U"><dt>Umpire</dt><dd>The neutral third member of an appraisal panel who decides items the two appraisers can’t settle.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-V"><dt>VPIA</dt><dd>The Virginia Property Insurance Association, Virginia’s FAIR Plan.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-X"><dt>Xactimate</dt><dd>Estimating software widely used by insurers and contractors to price repairs by region.</dd></div></dl></article><aside class="aside"><div class="aside-in"><div class="box box-call"><h2 class="box-h">Talk it through first</h2><p>The first conversation is free. We’ll tell you plainly whether appraisal fits your claim.</p><a class="box-tel" href="tel:+18445371427">+1 (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-accent btn-block" href="/schedule-a-claim-review/">Send claim details</a><p class="box-small">Mon–Fri 8am–6pm ET · All of Virginia</p></div><div class="box"><h2 class="box-h">Resources</h2><ul class="box-list"><li><a href="/virginia-appraisal-steps/">How appraisal works</a></li><li><a href="/virginia-appraisal-questions/">Appraisal FAQ</a></li><li><a href="/claim-and-policy-terms/" aria-current="page">Claim &amp; policy terms</a></li><li><a href="/policy-and-claim-guides/">Claim guides</a></li></ul></div></div></aside></div>
</main>
